  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Penan\"\nsubtitle: \"Malaysia\"\nregion: \"Southeast Asia\"\ntags: [ethnography, southeast-asia]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Penan are a nomadic Indigenous people. |\n| Where | Sarawak and Brunei in Borneo. |\n| How many | Around 16,000 people; about 200 still live nomadically. |\n| Language | Penan, also called Punan-Nibong, in the Kenyah subgroup of the Malayo-Polynesian branch of Austronesian. |\n| Religion | Originally animistic; many were Christianised, and half of Brunei’s Penan have converted to Islam. |\n| Known for | Hunting and gathering · Forest knowledge · Molong, taking no more than needed · Blowpipe hunting · Basketry and mats |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Penan live in eastern and central Borneo. They are divided into Eastern and Western groups. Most have settled since the 1950s, but a small number remain nomadic. Their culture is closely tied to hunting, gathering, forest knowledge and resistance to deforestation.\n\n## Material culture\n\nPenan material culture uses forest materials such as rattan, wood, animal parts, sago palm and tree latex.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nRattan mats, baskets and straps are important recorded Penan objects.\n\n- **Woven rattan mat** — A rectangular rattan mat has dark and undyed corners with bird, blow-pipe and moon patterns.\n- **Rattan basketry** — Recorded baskets are made from rattan with nylon or string.\n- **Rattan strap** — A rattan strap is listed among Penan museum objects.\n- **Penan fabric** — A 1957 eastern Penan textile was made from Musa textilis bast in brown, grey, black and blue-black.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nAnimal hides and other animal parts are used for clothing and shelter.\n\n- **Chawat loincloth** (*chawat*) — A poem refers to people wearing Chawats, described as loincloths.\n\n### Architecture\n\nPenan life shifted from forest nomadism to settled longhouse villages after 1950.\n\n- **Forest shelter** — A British Museum image shows Penan men resting in a shelter in Borneo.\n- **Longhouse village** — State programmes and Christian missionaries encouraged settlement in longhouse-based villages.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nThe blowpipe and forest knives are central recorded Penan tools.\n\n- **Blowpipe** (*kelepud*) — A kelepud is a three-metre wooden blowpipe made from Belian wood and bored with a bone drill.\n- **Blowpipe darts** — Darts are made from sago palm and tipped with poisonous Tajem-tree latex.\n- **Bush knife** (*tongol*) — A tongol has a wooden handle, rattan binding, metal blade and tree latex called mat.\n- **Parang** — A parang is made from metal, wood, rattan and gum.\n- **Knife with bone handle** — One recorded knife has a metal blade set into a long bone handle.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe sources describe sacred plants and animals, destroyed burial sites and major religious change.\n\n- **Sacred plants and animals** — Plants and animals may be seen as embodiments of powerful spirits and deities.\n- **Christianisation** — Missionaries of the Borneo Evangelical Mission baptised many Penan and encouraged settlement.\n- **Islamic conversion** — Half of the Penan community in Brunei has converted to Islam, even if only superficially.\n\n## Foodways\n\nPenan food comes mainly from hunting, gathering and the forest.\n\n- **Sago** — Sago is starch from the sago palm and is part of the Penan diet.\n- **Forest prey** — Hunted foods include wild boar, barking deer, mouse deer, monkeys, birds, frogs, monitor lizards, snails and locusts.\n- **Reticulated python** (*kemanen*) — The reticulated python, called kemanen, is among the hunted animals.\n- **Molong** — Molong means never taking more than necessary from the forest.\n- **Feel good** (*jian kenin*) — Jian kenin means “feel good” and is used in settled communities as an equivalent to “thank you.”\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nPenan communities use complaints, declarations, testimony and advocacy to defend their forests.\n\n- **Long Sayan Declaration** — The 2002 Long Sayan Declaration stated Penan wants and requirements to the Sarawak State Government.\n- **Forest testimony** — A Penan spokesman described the forest as the community’s money and bank because it provides food.\n- **Rumah Nor** — Rumah Nor is a documentary mentioned in the sources.\n- **Tawai: a Voice From The Forest** — Bruce Parry’s documentary Tawai: a Voice From The Forest is mentioned in the sources.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nPenan is a Kenyah-related language, while Penan religious life includes animism, Christianity and Islam.\n\n- **Penan language** (*Punan-Nibong*) — Penan is also known as Punan-Nibong and is spoken as a language complex in Borneo.\n- **Eastern and Western Penan languages** — Western Penan is closer to Sebop than to Eastern Penan.\n- **Animism** — The Penan were originally animistic hunter-gatherers who could view plants and animals as sacred.\n- **Borneo Evangelical Mission** — The Borneo Evangelical Mission Christianised, baptised and settled many Penan.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Punan-Nibong* — Another name for Penan\n- *kelepud* — Blowpipe\n- *tongol* — Bush knife\n- *mat* — Tree latex\n- *chawat* — Loincloth\n- *molong* — Taking no more than needed\n- *jian kenin* — Feel good\n- *kemanen* — Reticulated python\n- *parang* — Forest knife\n- *Belian* — Tree used for blowpipes\n- *Tajem* — Tree whose latex poisons darts\n- *Sebop* — Language related to Western Penan\n- *Musa textilis* — Plant used for bast fibre\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- The supplied sources mention Bruno Manser and the Bruno Manser Fonds, a non-profit organisation dedicated to the plight of the Penan.\n- [Penan people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penan_people)\n- [Penan language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penan_language)\n- No UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage inscription for this ethnic group is included in the supplied sources.\n- The supplied museum records are from the British Museum and the Museum of World Culture; the sources do not provide collection URLs.\n",
